{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "t41",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "excerpt": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "text": "clarify",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-07T22:12:14.764Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T22:13:39.767Z",
          "html": "<p>What T41 means, in plain words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    A library (say, a cards library) can make things of its own kinds: its deck makes cards with <code>a new card</code>.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    When Solitaire uses that library, you'd want the deck to make <em>Solitaire's</em> card (with Solitaire's own looks and\n    rules), not the library's plain one.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    Since P26 that happens, but only when the library's project file lists the type in its <code>exports</code>:\n    <code>\"exports\": [\"Card\", \"Deck\"]</code>. Without that line, the deck keeps making the library's own plain cards.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    I chose that so no existing project's compiled code changed. Making it the rule for every type would have changed 5\n    files in recipe-box, recetario, live-room and sala-en-vivo, for no gain.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The follow-up question T41 holds: is \"only what <code>exports</code> lists\" the rule you want, or should a library always let the project using it supply its own version of each type?</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changes unless you pick the other rule;  then every library type is made by name, and those 5 files' compiled code changes.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c8": {
      "anchor": "i91",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Solitaire's `pile` property of a card shows no compiled code with no sources",
      "excerpt": "Solitaire's `pile` property of a card shows no compiled code with no sources",
      "text": "fix?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T23:32:24.382Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T23:35:48.512Z",
          "html": "<p>It's fixed, and in main since this afternoon (merge 05788fba3).  <code>a card belongs to one pile</code> now compiles its <code>get pile()</code> inside Card with its comment and docstring, so the editor's scope explorer finds its code.  If you still see the problem, tell me where (a screenshot helps) and I'll look again.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c9": {
      "anchor": "i92",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "A half-typed tag in a drawing loses every action of the program",
      "excerpt": "A half-typed tag in a drawing loses every action of the program",
      "text": "fix",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T23:32:40.448Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T23:35:51.172Z",
          "html": "<p>It's fixed, and in main since this afternoon (merge 05788fba3).  Typing <code>&lt;action \"</code>, <code>&lt;action \"\"</code> or <code>&lt;text</code> in a drawing no longer loses the program's actions:  completing a quoted action word offers <code>auto play</code>, <code>cheat</code> and the rest, with the only error on the half-typed line.  Its end-tag twin (<code>&lt;/tex</code>) was fixed too, as I94.  If you still see it, tell me where and I'll look again.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c10": {
      "anchor": "j188",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "In a foundation's rule, `the pile` still doesn't mean the foundation",
      "excerpt": "In a foundation's rule, `the pile` still doesn't mean the foundation",
      "text": "seems like this should work.  Negative impact?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-15T17:38:46.244Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-15T17:39:59.393Z",
          "html": "<p>Little negative impact.  Making <code>the pile</code> mean the foundation inside a foundation's rules, as <code>the foundation</code> does, costs only these:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A name nearer in wins, as it does today:\n    <ul>\n      <li>a blank that's a pile, in an action on a foundation (<code>to give a card to a pile</code>):  <code>the pile</code> is the blank</li>\n      <li>a variable named <code>pile</code> (<code>for pile in the columns</code>):  <code>the pile</code> is the variable</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>A reader can mistake which pile is meant:  in a drop rule the card has its own pile too, which is why your draft says <code>the card's pile</code> for that one.</li>\n  <li>It goes up the kinds:  in a kind of foundation, <code>the foundation</code> and <code>the pile</code> both mean it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So I'll build it:  any kind it's a kind of names it, after blanks and variables.  It's queued after the <code>and</code> lines and J185, with the agent already working in that code.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-15T17:39:58.573Z"
      }
    }
  },
  "on": {}
}
